README-FORTRAN -- the RTF/68K Fortran-77 system, and the one thing it needs==========================================================================This disk carries a complete Fortran-77 compiler.  It went unnoticed for along time because every part of it appeared to do nothing at all: run `rtf`,`for`, `lnk`, `biory` or `creadoc` and you get silence and a prompt back.They are not broken.  Every one of them calls F$Link for a module named`os9lib`, gets E_MNF (module not found), and exits without printing anything.  $ /dd/CMDS/for  F$Link  A0="os9lib"  F$Link  returns #000:221 - E_MNF  F$Exit  D1.w=$00DD          <- 221, the same error, as the exit statusWHAT os9lib IS--------------`CMDS/os9lib` is the RTF Fortran RUN-TIME LIBRARY, not a program.  DOC/INDEXdescribed it as a support library all along, but a sweep that ran every filein CMDS as a program ran this one too, hit the floating-point code inside it,and recorded "wants a 68881".  Running a library as a program proves nothing.MAKING IT WORK--------------The module has to be in the module directory before anything can link it,and `load' is what puts it there.  CMDS/load is on this disk -- aclean-room reimplementation, source in SRC/load:  load /dd/CMDS/os9libVerified 2026-08-27.  With os9lib loaded the Fortran system runs; withnothing loaded every one of these programs prints nothing at all and exits.That silence is the whole symptom, and it reads like five broken programs.  for       " command: for file{,file} [-rtfopt{,rtfopt}] "  lnk       " command: lnk file{,file} [-l68opts] "  lnk.org   the same, and the same program  rtf       " command must be: RTF pathname [options..] "  biory     prompts for a name and a birth date, in German, and reads them  creadoc   extracts the documentation header of every .f file in the            current directory into creadoc.txt -- an early Fortran doc            generator.  It lists the directory with `dir -eadu' and reads            each file name from a fixed column.  It had fnpos=53 where            Microware's dir puts the name at 54 -- measured over 666 lines            in four directories, with sector addresses two to five digits            wide and sizes two to seven, the name sits at 54 in every one            -- so it opened `" bench.f"' with a leading space and stopped            with OPEN ERROR 214.  F$PrsNam follows the 68k manual and does            not skip a leading space.  That constant reads 54 now, in            SRC/rtf/creadoc.f and in the module here, and it walks every            file.  DOC/rtf/biory.doc is the output it produced for biory.f.HOW FAR YOU CAN GET-------------------  COMPILING WORKS.  `rtf /h6/prime.f` on the demo in SRC/rtf reports    " Total Errors   0   Warnings   0 / RTF normally completed "  and writes `prime.a`.  ASSEMBLING AND LINKING TAKE YOUR OWN OS-9's r68 AND l68.  rtf emits  68000 ASSEMBLY, not an object file, and your system's assembler and  linker finish the job.  SYS/login makes r68, l68, del and the shell  resident from /h1.  Measured 2026-09-22, the whole chain on fact.f:    load /dd/CMDS/os9lib    rtf fact.f                       compiles to fact.a    r68 -q fact.a -o=fact.r          r68 wants your DEFS/oskdefs.d    r68 -q rtfstart.a -o=rtfstart.r  the start-up code, SRC/rtf/rtfstart.a    l68 rtfstart.r fact.r -l=sys.l -o=fact    fact                             prints 1! to 12!  r68 reads `oskdefs.d' from the directory it runs in, and sys.l is your  own library; copy both beside the source first.  l68 writes the program  to your execution directory.  The drivers do the same with less typing.  `for fact' runs rtf, r68 and  del in turn.  `lnk fact' links fact.r, and looks for sys.l and the  start-up code in LIB on /h0 -- where its authors' systems kept them --  and writes the program to CMDS there; `lnk.org' is the same but calls  the start-up code fstart.r.  So assemble rtfstart.a into /h0/LIB under  the name your driver wants and copy your sys.l beside it.  The cards  for fact, for, lnk and lnk.org show each of these running.  The .as0/as1/as4/as5/as11 assemblers on this disk are 6800, 6801,  6804, 6805 and 68HC11 cross-assemblers and cannot assemble 68k code.WHAT IS HERE------------  DOC/rtf/rtfman.txt    the RTF/68K manual, 227 KB, compiler and run-time                        library, version 2.13, 12 March 1987  DOC/rtf/biory.doc     documentation creadoc generated from biory.f --  DOC/rtf/creadoc.doc   which is also the demonstration of what creadoc does  SRC/rtf/*.f           biory, creadoc, and five demos: prime, bench,                        float, div, vtest  SRC/rtf/rtfstart.a    the start-up code lnk wants, and its 68020 variantRTF/68K is a CERN product.  Its manual states the package is distributedfree.  The compiler, its data module, the library and the two examples cameto this collection from EFFO public-domain disk 1 and EFFO forum disk 5.